{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_12","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '1,260p' /home/lixinyu/snap/a/des/seed2094082456/11/prompt.md\"","aggregated_output":"你是一个 ARC-AGI 谜题设计专家。下面的 20 道参考题是难度和机制质量的标杆。仔细阅读每道题的**解题逻辑**（解题者从样例归纳规则的认知过程），而非表面场景。你的新题应在机制深度和隐蔽性上与参考题持平或者实现超越。\n\n每个参考题描述包含：\n- `description`：一句话概述题目的变换目标。\n- `Input structure`：输入网格中有哪些对象、背景、标记、边界或组件。\n- `Rule`：从输入到输出的确定性变换规则。\n- `Key insight`：解题所需的核心洞见。\n\n参考题目如下：\n<reference_puzzle index=\"1\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1a244afd\" lines=\"49-68\">\n<description>\nRotate each magenta source pixel 90 degrees counterclockwise around its matched blue pivot, recoloring the new pixel orange.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- The only foreground colors are blue pivot pixels and magenta source pixels.\n- Each magenta source can be paired one-to-one with a blue pivot in the same\n  row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Remove all magenta source pixels.\n- For each matched source/pivot pair, preserve the source distance from the\n  pivot but rotate its offset 90 degrees counterclockwise.\n- Paint the rotated in-bounds target orange; if the target is outside the\n  grid, the source simply disappears.\n\nKey insight:\nThe blue pixels are rotation centers, and row/column alignment gives a\nunique global matching from each magenta source to its pivot.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"2\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"30f42897\" lines=\"51-66\">\n<description>\nRepeat the single colored border segment around the rectangular border, leaving one segment-length gap between copies.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- There is exactly one foreground color.\n- All foreground pixels form one contiguous segment on the rectangular border, possibly crossing the cycle start.\n\nRule:\n- Unwrap the grid border clockwise into a cyclic path.\n- Let n be the length of the foreground segment.\n- Copy the segment with the same color every 2*n border positions around the cycle.\n\nKey insight:\n- The foreground segment is not extended by rows or columns directly; it is a motif on the perimeter cycle, repeated with an equal-sized gap.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"3\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"aaecdb9a\" lines=\"10-26\">\n<description>\nConvert scattered foreground objects into bottom-aligned count bars in a fixed five-color order.\n\nInput structure\nThe grid has a dominant background color and foreground objects in the task\npalette: grey, red, teal, maroon, and pink. Objects are monochromatic\n8-connected components; some palette colors may be absent.\n\nRule\nFor each palette color in order grey, red, teal, maroon, pink, count its\n8-connected objects. Create a background grid with five columns and height\nequal to the largest count, then fill each column upward from the bottom\nwith its palette color for that count.\n\nKey insight\nThe output does not preserve object shapes or positions; it is a histogram\nof per-color 8-connected object counts, using the learned palette order.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"4\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"87ab05b8\" lines=\"9-24\">\n<description>\nErase all distractor pixels and expand the single red control pixel into the grid quadrant that contains it.\n\nInput structure:\n- The grid has one dominant background color.\n- Exactly one red pixel is the control pixel.\n- Other non-background pixels are distractors.\n\nRule:\n- Clear the canvas to the background color.\n- Split the grid into equal top/bottom and left/right halves.\n- Fill the quadrant containing the red control pixel with red.\n\nKey insight:\n- The red control pixel selects a whole half-grid quadrant by its row half and column half; all other foreground pixels are noise.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"5\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"f8cc533f\" lines=\"10-25\">\n<description>\nComplete every colored sprite by applying the union of all aligned partial sprite masks.\n\nInput structure:\n- The dominant color is the background.\n- Each non-background color appears as a separated partial copy of one common sprite mask.\n- The copies share a top-left-aligned sprite window; some copies may be missing lower or right edge pixels.\n\nRule:\n- Crop each same-color mask to its bounding box.\n- Pad the crops to the maximum observed height and width, aligned at the top-left, and OR them into one complete template.\n- Fill that template at every sprite location using the sprite's own color.\n\nKey insight:\n- No single input sprite is complete, but their missing cells are complementary, so the aligned union reveals the full mask.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"6\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"1efba499\" lines=\"70-85\">\n<description>\nSwap paired marker pixels across the largest foreground-color barrier, placing them just outside the barrier segment.\n\nInput structure:\n- The background is the dominant color.\n- One foreground color is the unique most frequent non-background color; its pixels form the stationary barrier.\n- Other foreground pixels are single markers, sometimes paired on opposite sides of one contiguous barrier segment in the same row or column.\n\nRule:\n- Scan each row and column that crosses exactly one contiguous barrier segment.\n- If the line has exactly one marker on each side of that segment, erase both source markers and place each marker in the cell immediately beyond the opposite side of the segment.\n- Lines with markers on only one side are unchanged; the barrier remains unchanged.\n\nKey insight:\n- The marker pairing is local to a row or column, and the destination is defined by the nearest cells outside the intervening barrier segment, not by the original distance from the barrier.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"7\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"7b3084d4\" lines=\"253-266\">\n<description>\nAssemble colored rectangular sprites into a single tiled rectangle, anchored by a marker pixel.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Several solid-colored rectangular sprites scattered on the grid.\n- One sprite has a unique single-pixel marker (different color) at one corner — this is the anchor.\n\nRule:\n- Identify the anchor sprite (contains the marker pixel) and the remaining pieces.\n- Rotate the anchor so the marker pixel lands at position (0,0) of the output.\n- Pack all remaining pieces (allowing 90° rotations) to tile a complete rectangle with no gaps.\n\nKey insight: The lone marker pixel picks the anchor and fixes the output orientation — it must sit at the top-left corner.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"8\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"221dfab4\" lines=\"6-19\">\n<description>\nPropagate a repeating striped pattern from a yellow border segment toward the opposite edge, recoloring foreground objects on certain stripes.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color. Foreground blobs of another color scattered in the interior.\n- A short yellow (4) segment sits on one border edge.\n\nRule:\n- From the yellow segment, march toward the opposite edge in steps of 2 cells, cycling colors Y, Y, G (4, 4, 3). The 1-cell gap between stripes is forced to bg.\n- Yellow stripes paint only within the segment's span.\n- Green stripes additionally recolor all non-bg pixels across the entire row/column to green.\n\nKey insight: Green stripes act as a \"flood\" that recolors existing objects on the full row/column, while yellow stripes are confined to the segment span.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"9\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"edb79dae\" lines=\"39-53\">\n<description>\nStamp recolored sprites into a framed region using placeholder positions and a color legend.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg background, a rectangular single-color frame, colored placeholder cells inside the frame.\n- Outside the frame: sprite shapes (one per placeholder color) and a small legend mapping each placeholder color to an output color.\n\nRule:\n- Find the frame and its interior placeholders.\n- For each placeholder color, locate the matching-color sprite outside the frame.\n- Stamp each sprite at the placeholder's position inside the frame.\n- Recolor every stamped sprite to the output color defined by the legend.\n\nKey insight: The legend pairs (overlapping two-color glyphs outside the frame) dictate the final recoloring — placeholder color selects the shape, legend maps it to the output color.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"10\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"800d221b\" lines=\"7-22\">\n<description>\nRecolor a path-network by adjacent seed regions.\n\nInput structure:\n- bg = most common color.\n- Path color P forms a thin orthogonal network with a solid square hub (NxN).\n- Seed colors appear in colored regions (non-bg, non-P) adjacent to path branches.\n\nRule:\n- Infer P as the non-bg color whose largest component is most network-like.\n- Find the largest solid k×k all-P square (the hub); exclude it from recoloring.\n- Recolor each path branch to the seed-region color it touches most.\n- Recolor the hub center pixel to the seed whose branches touch the hub most.\n\nKey insight: The hub stays uncolored; branches inherit color from adjacent seed regions by adjacency count.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"11\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"dfadab01\" lines=\"79-93\">\n<description>\nExpand each colored control pixel into its corresponding fixed 4x4 glyph.\n\nInput structure:\n- Black background with single-pixel markers in colors 2, 3, 5, and/or 8.\n- Some inputs also show a sample 4x4 glyph in the output color, with a same-rule marker just below-right of the sample.\n\nRule:\n- Replace each marker color by its learned 4x4 glyph: 2->yellow frame, 3->blue ring, 5->pink diagonal blocks, 8->orange X.\n- Paint each glyph with its top-left corner at the marker pixel, clipping at the grid boundary.\n- Remove the sample glyphs and skip their below-right label markers.\n\nKey insight:\n- The colored pixels are control pixels for a fixed glyph alphabet; non-control sample glyphs are only legend entries, not preserved scene objects.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"12\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"d8e07eb2\" lines=\"29-47\">\n<description>\nLook up query glyphs in a reference table and mark whether they form a line.\n\nInput structure:\n- Teal background with two full-width pink separator rows.\n- Above the first separator is a query row of 5x5 glyph cells.\n- Between separators is a reference table of the same 5x5 cells; a glyph is\n  identified by both its color and its 3x3 shape.\n\nRule:\n- Green-fill the background inside every table cell whose glyph exactly\n  appears in the query row.\n- If the matched table cells form one complete row or one complete column,\n  also green-fill the query band and the bottom verdict band.\n- Otherwise leave the query band unchanged and fill the bottom verdict band red.\n\nKey insight: The bottom color is not a count; it is the yes/no answer to\nwhether the queried glyphs align as a full row or column in the table.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"13\" source=\"train\" puzzle_id=\"e45ef808\" lines=\"31-46\">\n<description>\nMark the shallowest and deepest columns of a solid terrain boundary with vertical colored guide lines.\n\nInput structure:\n- The first row is a solid empty separator.\n- Below it, each column consists of upper fill cells followed by a contiguous suffix of terrain cells.\n- The bottom row is all terrain, so every column has a well-defined first terrain row.\n\nRule:\n- Find the column whose first terrain row is latest; it is unique and its upper fill run becomes maroon.\n- Find the rightmost column whose first terrain row is earliest; its upper fill run becomes yellow.\n- Preserve the empty separator, terrain cells, and all other upper fill cells.\n\nKey insight:\n- The task is a column-height summary of the terrain profile: color the deepest upper run and the highest terrain peak.\n</description>\n</reference_puzzle>\n\n<reference_puzzle index=\"14\" source=\"eval\" puzzle_id=\"bf45cf4b\" lines=\"7-19\">\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
